window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url":"index.html","page":"Aptitude & Reasoning Field Guide","section":"Crack the aptitude round.","tool":"home","text":"Crack the aptitude round. A sharp, visual path through the online-assessment round that gates tech interviews — quantitative aptitude, logical reasoning, verbal ability, and data interpretation . Every topic gets a plain-English idea, a diagram, the shortcut, and a timed drill so you build real spee","keywords":["crack","the","aptitude","round"]},{"url":"index.html#progress","page":"Aptitude & Reasoning Field Guide","section":"Your progress","tool":"home","text":"01 Your progress Mark a lesson \"learned\" as you finish it — progress is saved in this browser. Pick up where you left off, or jump straight to a section below. Start learning Reset progress","keywords":["your","progress"]},{"url":"index.html#path","page":"Aptitude & Reasoning Field Guide","section":"The path to exam-ready","tool":"home","text":"02 The path to exam-ready Aptitude is a speed skill, not a knowledge dump: learn the shortcut , drill it under time , then mix and race the clock until the easy marks are automatic. Step 1 · build the base Strategy & speed math Learn how the test is scored and the mental-math kit that powers everyth","keywords":["the","path","to","exam","ready"]},{"url":"index.html#modules","page":"Aptitude & Reasoning Field Guide","section":"Explore the sections","tool":"home","text":"03 Explore the sections Four exam sections, a foundations primer, a practice arena, and a reference shelf. Start at the top or dive into whatever you're weakest at. Start here Strategy & speed How the OA is scored, the four-step solve method, and the mental-math toolkit. Start -> Numbers Quantitativ","keywords":["explore","the","sections"]},{"url":"foundations/how-aptitude-tests-work.html","page":"How aptitude tests work","section":"How aptitude tests work.","tool":"foundations","text":"How aptitude tests work. Before any shortcut, know the battlefield. The online assessment (OA) that gates a tech interview isn't a maths exam — it's a speed-and-triage game. Win it by playing the format, not just the questions.","keywords":["how","aptitude","tests","work"]},{"url":"foundations/how-aptitude-tests-work.html#anatomy","page":"How aptitude tests work","section":"Anatomy of a placement OA","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Anatomy of a placement OA A typical OA bundles four sections — Quantitative Aptitude, Logical Reasoning, Verbal Ability, Data Interpretation — under a hard clock, with roughly 30–60 seconds per question . You almost never finish everything. The score that clears the cut-off comes from grabbing th","keywords":["anatomy","of","placement","oa"]},{"url":"foundations/how-aptitude-tests-work.html#method","page":"How aptitude tests work","section":"The universal solve method","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The universal solve method Every question, every section: spot the type → recall the shortcut → eliminate options → commit (guess smart or skip) . Same four beats, on repeat. 1 Spot the type Read the first line and name it: \"this is successive-percentage\", \"this is a blood-relation tree\". Recogni","keywords":["the","universal","solve","method"]},{"url":"foundations/how-aptitude-tests-work.html#triage","page":"How aptitude tests work","section":"Time triage: the 90-second rule","tool":"foundations","text":"03 Time triage: the 90-second rule Do two passes . Pass 1: sweep the section and answer only what falls in under ~40s. Pass 2: return to the flagged ones with whatever time is left. Cap any single question at about 90 seconds . Hit the cap with no clear path? Eliminate what you can, mark it, and lea","keywords":["time","triage","the","second","rule"]},{"url":"foundations/how-aptitude-tests-work.html#solve","page":"How aptitude tests work","section":"Play it fast","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Play it fast Negative marking is ON (−¼ per wrong). You've cut a question down to 2 plausible options. Guess or skip? warm-up Decide it Down to 2, your guess wins half the time. Expected value = ½(+1) + ½(−¼) = +⅜ per such guess — positive, so guess . The fast way shortcut Rule of thumb with −¼ m","keywords":["play","it","fast"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"The speed-math toolkit.","tool":"foundations","text":"The speed-math toolkit. Under a 40-second clock, long multiplication loses. This is the mental-math kit the toppers run on autopilot — a handful of tricks that turn ugly arithmetic into one clean step.","keywords":["the","speed","math","toolkit"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html#idea","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"Why tricks beat arithmetic","tool":"foundations","text":"01 Why tricks beat arithmetic Almost every \"scary\" calculation hides an easy reshape : ×5 is really ÷2, ×25 is really ÷4, 12.5% is really ÷8. Learn the reshape and you skip the grind. You won't compute faster than the clock — you'll compute less . The goal isn't speed-arithmetic; it's spotting the s","keywords":["why","tricks","beat","arithmetic"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html#multiply","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"The multiplication tricks","tool":"foundations","text":"02 The multiplication tricks Four reshapes cover most of what the exam throws at you. Each replaces a multiplication with a halving, a quartering, or a tiny addition. ×5 = ÷2 then ×10 # 86 × 5 → 43 → 430 ×25 = ÷4 then ×100 # 36 × 25 → 9 → 900 ×11 = write the digit, then each neighbour-sum, then the ","keywords":["the","multiplication","tricks"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html#fractions","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"%↔fraction table + approximation + unit digits","tool":"foundations","text":"03 %↔fraction table + approximation + unit digits Memorise the percent-to-fraction map cold — a percentage problem becomes a single division . Then approximate to land the order of magnitude, and use unit-digit cycles when only the last digit matters. Percent Fraction To find it 50% · 25% 1/2 · 1/4 ","keywords":["fraction","table","approximation","unit","digits"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html#backsolve","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"Back-solve from the options","tool":"foundations","text":"04 Back-solve from the options It's MCQ — the answer is already on the screen. When the algebra is slow, plug the options in and see which one fits. Start from the middle choice to halve your tries. Pair this with elimination: kill any option with the wrong sign, wrong unit, or wrong last digit, the","keywords":["back","solve","from","the","options"]},{"url":"foundations/speed-math-toolkit.html#solve","page":"The speed-math toolkit","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"foundations","text":"05 Solve it fast Compute 25 × 16 quickly. warm-up Solve it ×25 = ÷4 then ×100. 16 ÷ 4 = 4 → 400 . The fast way shortcut Never long-multiply by 25 — it's a quarter of 100. Divide by 4 and tack on two zeros. Same for ×50 (÷2, ×100) and ×125 (÷8, ×1000). Find 45². core Solve it Number ends in 5, so n =","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"Numbers & divisibility.","tool":"quant","text":"Numbers & divisibility. The bedrock of every quant section — divisibility shortcuts, HCF/LCM, remainders and last digits. None of it needs long division if you know the tricks.","keywords":["numbers","divisibility"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html#idea","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea Every whole number is a product of primes . Once you see that skeleton, divisibility, HCF (shared primes, lowest powers) and LCM (all primes, highest powers) fall out for free. The exam never wants the long division — it wants you to test divisibility on sight, combine factors, or read a","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html#divisibility","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"Divisibility rules on sight","tool":"quant","text":"02 Divisibility rules on sight Don't divide — test . Each rule reads a number's digits, not the whole value. Combine two coprime rules to cover a bigger divisor. By Rule Example 2 last digit even 1 4 ✓ 3 digit sum divisible by 3 17 1 → 9 ✓ 4 last two digits ÷ 4 3 16 ✓ 5 ends in 0 or 5 23 5 ✓ 8 last ","keywords":["divisibility","rules","on","sight"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html#hcf-lcm","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"HCF & LCM by prime factors","tool":"quant","text":"03 HCF & LCM by prime factors Factorise both numbers. HCF = common primes at their lowest power; LCM = every prime at its highest power. Their product always equals a × b. 24 = 2 3 ·3 36 = 2 2 ·3 2 → HCF = 2 2 ·3 = 12 , LCM = 2 3 ·3 2 = 72 12 HCF (low powers) 72 LCM (high powers) 864 12 × 72 = 24 × ","keywords":["hcf","lcm","by","prime","factors"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html#remainders","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"Remainders & last digits","tool":"quant","text":"04 Remainders & last digits For powers, don't compute the giant number — find the cycle . The unit digit of a n repeats every 1–4 powers; the remainder of a n ÷ m repeats too. Base ends in Unit-digit cycle Period 2 2, 4, 8, 6 4 3 3, 9, 7, 1 4 7 7, 9, 3, 1 4 4 / 9 4,6 · 9,1 2 0,1,5,6 itself 1 Pro tip","keywords":["remainders","last","digits"]},{"url":"quant/numbers-and-divisibility.html#solve","page":"Numbers & divisibility","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"05 Solve it fast HCF of 24 and 36? warm-up Solve it 24 = 2 3 ·3, 36 = 2 2 ·3 2 . Common primes at lowest power: 2 2 ·3 = 12 . The fast way shortcut Subtract-and-repeat (Euclid): 36 − 24 = 12, and 12 divides 24 → HCF = 12. No factorising needed. Remainder when 2 10 is divided by 7? core Solve it 2 3 ","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/percentages.html","page":"Percentages","section":"Percentages.","tool":"quant","text":"Percentages. The single most-tested quant idea — and the engine behind profit/loss, interest, and data interpretation. Master the fraction conversions and you'll answer most of these in your head.","keywords":["percentages"]},{"url":"quant/percentages.html#idea","page":"Percentages","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea A percent is just a fraction with denominator 100. \"x% of y\" = (x/100) × y — and crucially, x% of y = y% of x . Percentages let you compare unlike things on one scale. The exam reward isn't the definition — it's converting the ugly ones to clean fractions so you never long-multiply. In p","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/percentages.html#fractions","page":"Percentages","section":"Memorise the fraction table","tool":"quant","text":"02 Memorise the fraction table Pros don't compute 37.5% of 64 — they see 3/8 × 64 = 24 . These conversions turn arithmetic into one division. Learn them cold. Percent Fraction Trick 50% / 25% 1/2 · 1/4 halve / quarter 20% / 10% / 5% 1/5 · 1/10 · 1/20 ÷5 · ÷10 · ÷20 12.5% / 37.5% 1/8 · 3/8 ÷8 then sc","keywords":["memorise","the","fraction","table"]},{"url":"quant/percentages.html#successive","page":"Percentages","section":"Successive change = multiply, don't add","tool":"quant","text":"03 Successive change = multiply, don't add The number-one percentage trap: a 20% rise then a 20% fall is not a wash. Stack changes by multiplying their factors — a +20% is ×1.2, a −20% is ×0.8. final = start × (1 ± a /100) × (1 ± b /100) # 1.2 × 0.8 = 0.96 → 4% net FALL The trap (adding) +20% then −","keywords":["successive","change","multiply","don","add"]},{"url":"quant/percentages.html#solve","page":"Percentages","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast What is 37.5% of 64? warm-up Solve it 37.5% = 3/8. So 3/8 × 64 = 3 × 8 = 24 . The fast way shortcut See the fraction, not the decimal: 64 ÷ 8 = 8, ×3 = 24. No long multiplication. A number increased by 25% becomes 75. The original number? core Solve it 1.25 × n = 75 -> n = 75 / 1.25","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures.","tool":"quant","text":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures. Ratios share out a whole, proportions cross-multiply, and alligation cracks any \"what blend gives this average\" question on a single see-saw.","keywords":["ratio","proportion","mixtures"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html#idea","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea A ratio a : b is just parts . To split a total, add the parts and find one part ; everything else is multiples of it. Proportion means two ratios are equal — so you can cross-multiply . Mixtures ask which ratio two strengths combine in; alligation answers that without algebra. In plain E","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html#chain-proportion","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"Chaining ratios & proportion","tool":"quant","text":"02 Chaining ratios & proportion To join a : b and b : c, make the shared term match (scale to its LCM), then read a : c straight off. Proportion problems cross-multiply. 1 Spot the shared term a:b = 2:3 and b:c = 4:5 share b (3 vs 4). 2 Scale to match LCM(3,4)=12: a:b = 8:12, b:c = 12:15. 3 Read acr","keywords":["chaining","ratios","proportion"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html#partnership","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"Partnership: capital × time","tool":"quant","text":"03 Partnership: capital × time When partners invest different amounts for different durations, profit splits in the ratio of capital × time — not capital alone. share i ∝ capital i × months i # ₹6000·12 : ₹9000·8 = 72 : 72 = 1 : 1 72k A: 6000 × 12 72k B: 9000 × 8 1 : 1 profit split","keywords":["partnership","capital","time"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html#alligation","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"Mixtures & alligation (the see-saw)","tool":"quant","text":"04 Mixtures & alligation (the see-saw) To hit a mean by blending a cheaper and a dearer item, the distances from the mean cross over . The ratio is (dearer − mean) : (mean − cheaper). Alligation see-saw — blend ₹12 & ₹18 to ₹16 Cross the distances: the cheaper's share sits under the dearer gap. Rati","keywords":["mixtures","alligation","the","see","saw"]},{"url":"quant/ratio-proportion-mixtures.html#solve","page":"Ratio, proportion & mixtures","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"05 Solve it fast Divide 60 in the ratio 2 : 3. warm-up Solve it Parts = 2 + 3 = 5, so one part = 60 ÷ 5 = 12. Shares = 2×12 and 3×12 = 24 and 36 . The fast way shortcut Always: total ÷ (sum of parts) = one part, then scale. No equations, no x. If a : b = 2 : 3 and b : c = 4 : 5, find a : c. core Sol","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/averages-and-ages.html","page":"Averages & ages","section":"Averages & ages.","tool":"quant","text":"Averages & ages. Average is just sum over count — but the speed comes from shifting the whole set at once, and from knowing that age gaps never change.","keywords":["averages","ages"]},{"url":"quant/averages-and-ages.html#idea","page":"Averages & ages","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea Average = sum ÷ count . So sum = average × count — and if you add k to every term, the mean rises by k . Most \"find the missing value\" questions are really \"recover the sum\". Get the total, subtract what you know, done. In plain English analogy The average is the water level if you poure","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/averages-and-ages.html#weighted","page":"Averages & ages","section":"Weighted average = alligation","tool":"quant","text":"02 Weighted average = alligation When groups have different sizes, you can't average the averages directly — weight each by its count. That's exactly the alligation see-saw from mixtures. weighted avg = n 1 ·a 1 + n 2 ·a 2 n 1 + n 2 # the mean lands nearer the bigger group 30 → 60 boys: 30, avg 60 2","keywords":["weighted","average","alligation"]},{"url":"quant/averages-and-ages.html#ages","page":"Averages & ages","section":"Ages: the gap is frozen","tool":"quant","text":"03 Ages: the gap is frozen Everyone ages at the same rate, so the difference between two ages never changes. Set the present unknown as x, write each person in terms of x, and shift by the same amount for past/future. Father 40, son 10 — when is father twice the son? The 30-year gap never moves. Twi","keywords":["ages","the","gap","is","frozen"]},{"url":"quant/averages-and-ages.html#solve","page":"Averages & ages","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast Average of 30 numbers is 20. Add 5 to each. New average? warm-up Solve it Adding a constant to every term adds it straight to the mean: 20 + 5 = 25 . The fast way shortcut The count (30) is a distractor — a uniform shift moves the mean by the shift, full stop. Ignore n. Average of 4","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/profit-loss-and-interest.html","page":"Profit, loss & interest","section":"Profit, loss & interest.","tool":"quant","text":"Profit, loss & interest. Commerce sums are easy marks once you fix the base: profit % rides on cost, discount rides on marked price, and CI beats SI by the interest-on-interest.","keywords":["profit","loss","interest"]},{"url":"quant/profit-loss-and-interest.html#idea","page":"Profit, loss & interest","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea Profit or loss % is always measured on the cost price . Selling price = cost × (1 ± profit%/100). Every commerce question hinges on which number is the base. Name cost price (CP), marked price (MP) and selling price (SP) before touching a percentage. In plain English analogy Think of cos","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/profit-loss-and-interest.html#mp-discount","page":"Profit, loss & interest","section":"Marked price & discount","tool":"quant","text":"02 Marked price & discount Shops mark up, then discount. Discount is a cut off the marked price (MP), not the cost. SP = MP × (1 − discount%/100), and profit is still judged against CP. SP = MP × (1 − d /100) # ₹250 marked, 20% off → 250 × 0.8 = 200 Common trap gotcha Two bases, two traps: profit % ","keywords":["marked","price","discount"]},{"url":"quant/profit-loss-and-interest.html#si-ci","page":"Profit, loss & interest","section":"Simple vs compound interest","tool":"quant","text":"03 Simple vs compound interest Simple interest is the same flat amount each year. Compound interest earns interest on past interest , so it pulls ahead — first noticeably in year 2. SI = P · R · T 100 A = P(1 + R /100) n # CI interest = A − P Simple interest ₹1000 at 10% for 2 yrs: 100 + 100 = ₹200 ","keywords":["simple","vs","compound","interest"]},{"url":"quant/profit-loss-and-interest.html#solve","page":"Profit, loss & interest","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast A marked price of ₹250 carries a 20% discount. You pay? warm-up Solve it Discount is off the marked price: 250 × 0.8 = ₹200 . The fast way shortcut 20% off = pay ⅘ . 250 × ⅘ = 200 in one step — never compute the discount, then subtract. An item sold at ₹90 gives a 10% loss. Its cost","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"Time, speed & distance.","tool":"quant","text":"Time, speed & distance. One triangle — speed, distance, time — powers trains, boats, and races. Nail the unit switch and \"add or subtract speeds\" rule, and these turn into 30-second wins.","keywords":["time","speed","distance"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html#idea","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea Everything hangs off one relation: speed = distance ÷ time . Cover the one you want with your thumb and the other two show the formula. The exam rarely asks the raw formula. It hides it inside trains, rivers, and two-person chases — so the real skill is the unit switch and knowing when s","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html#units","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"The unit switch: ×5/18 and ×18/5","tool":"quant","text":"02 The unit switch: ×5/18 and ×18/5 Trains are timed in seconds but quoted in km/h — so you must convert. Multiply km/h by 5/18 to get m/s, and m/s by 18/5 to go back. Memorise it like a reflex. km/h → m/s : × 5 /18 | m/s → km/h : × 18 /5 # 72 × 5/18 = 20 m/s 72 km/h = 20 m/s 36 km/h = 10 m/s 54 km/","keywords":["the","unit","switch","and"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html#relative","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"Relative speed & trains crossing","tool":"quant","text":"03 Relative speed & trains crossing Two movers? Use one frame. Opposite directions add the speeds; same direction subtracts them. A train crossing an object travels its own length + the object's length . Opposite = add · Same = subtract Head-on: 60 + 40 = 100 km/h closing speed. Chasing: 60 − 40 = 2","keywords":["relative","speed","trains","crossing"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html#boats-average","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"Boats & streams + average speed","tool":"quant","text":"04 Boats & streams + average speed A river just adds or removes the boat's speed. Downstream = boat + stream , upstream = boat − stream . For a there-and-back trip at two speeds, average speed is harmonic : 2xy ÷ (x+y). downstream = b + s · upstream = b − s # b = boat in still water, s = stream avg ","keywords":["boats","streams","average","speed"]},{"url":"quant/time-speed-distance.html#solve","page":"Time, speed & distance","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"05 Solve it fast Convert 90 km/h to m/s. warm-up Solve it × 5/18: 90 × 5 ÷ 18 = 450 ÷ 18 = 25 m/s . The fast way shortcut 90 ÷ 18 = 5 first, then × 5 = 25. Cancel before you multiply — never compute 450 ÷ 18 the long way. A boat does 10 km/h in still water; the stream is 2 km/h. Find downstream and ","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/time-and-work.html","page":"Time & work","section":"Time & work.","tool":"quant","text":"Time & work. \"A does it in 10 days, B in 15 — together?\" The trap is to average days. The fix is to add rates , or better, run the LCM trick and work in clean whole units.","keywords":["time","work"]},{"url":"quant/time-and-work.html#idea","page":"Time & work","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea If someone finishes a job in n days, their rate is 1/n per day . Working together, you add the rates — never the days. Days don't add; speeds do. Convert each worker to \"fraction of the job per day\", sum those fractions, then flip the total back to get the days the team needs. In plain E","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/time-and-work.html#lcm-trick","page":"Time & work","section":"The LCM trick — kill the fractions","tool":"quant","text":"02 The LCM trick — kill the fractions Adding fractions is slow. Instead, let total work = LCM of the days . Then each person does (total ÷ their days) whole units per day — clean integers all the way to the answer. 1 Set total work = LCM(days) A in 10, B in 15 → total work = LCM(10, 15) = 30 units .","keywords":["the","lcm","trick","kill","fractions"]},{"url":"quant/time-and-work.html#pipes","page":"Time & work","section":"Pipes & cisterns + M·D/W","tool":"quant","text":"03 Pipes & cisterns + M·D/W Pipes are time-and-work in disguise: an inlet adds to the rate, an outlet (leak) subtracts . And for staffing, workers × days ÷ work stays constant. net rate = inlets − outlets # fill 1/4, empty 1/6 → 1/4 − 1/6 = 1/12 → 12 h M 1 · D 1 W 1 = M 2 · D 2 W 2 # men · days per ","keywords":["pipes","cisterns"]},{"url":"quant/time-and-work.html#solve","page":"Time & work","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast A does a job in 10 days, B in 15. What fraction do they finish in one day together? warm-up Solve it 1/10 + 1/15 = 3/30 + 2/30 = 5/30 = 1 6 per day, so the whole job takes 6 days. The fast way shortcut LCM(10,15) = 30: A = 3/day, B = 2/day, sum 5/day → 5/30 = 1/6, and 30 ÷ 5 = 6 day","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/permutations-combinations-probability.html","page":"Permutations, combinations & probability","section":"Permutations, combinations & probability.","tool":"quant","text":"Permutations, combinations & probability. Counting questions are decided by one choice up front: does order matter? Get that right and nPr, nCr, and basic probability all fall out of multiplying choices.","keywords":["permutations","combinations","probability"]},{"url":"quant/permutations-combinations-probability.html#idea","page":"Permutations, combinations & probability","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea The fundamental counting principle : if step 1 has m ways and step 2 has n ways, together they have m × n ways. Everything else is bookkeeping on top of this. A factorial n! = n × (n−1) × … × 1 just counts the ways to arrange n distinct items in a line. From there, ordering picks (permut","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/permutations-combinations-probability.html#perm-vs-comb","page":"Permutations, combinations & probability","section":"Permutation vs combination","tool":"quant","text":"02 Permutation vs combination Same pool, one question: does the order count? If yes, it's a permutation (a lineup photo); if no, it's a combination (picking a team). nPr is always bigger — it counts each group r! times. Permutation — order matters A \"lineup photo\": gold, silver, bronze. ABC ≠ BCA. U","keywords":["permutation","vs","combination"]},{"url":"quant/permutations-combinations-probability.html#probability","page":"Permutations, combinations & probability","section":"Basic probability — dice, coins, cards","tool":"quant","text":"03 Basic probability — dice, coins, cards For equally-likely outcomes, P(event) = favourable ÷ total . Count the favourable outcomes, count all outcomes, divide. P(event) = favourable outcomes total outcomes # 0 ≤ P ≤ 1 Setup Total outcomes Example One die 6 P(even) = 3/6 = ½ Two coins 4 (HH, HT, TH","keywords":["basic","probability","dice","coins","cards"]},{"url":"quant/permutations-combinations-probability.html#solve","page":"Permutations, combinations & probability","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast In how many ways can the letters of CAT be arranged? warm-up Solve it 3 distinct letters in a row → 3! = 3 × 2 × 1 = 6 . The fast way shortcut \"Arrange all distinct items\" is just n!. For 3 letters that's 6 — no formula juggling. (Repeated letters would divide by the repeats' factor","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"quant/geometry-and-mensuration.html","page":"Geometry & mensuration","section":"Geometry & mensuration.","tool":"quant","text":"Geometry & mensuration. Easy marks if you know the formula and a Pythagorean triple. Most OA geometry is area/perimeter/volume plus a right triangle — not olympiad proofs.","keywords":["geometry","mensuration"]},{"url":"quant/geometry-and-mensuration.html#idea","page":"Geometry & mensuration","section":"The idea","tool":"quant","text":"01 The idea Mensuration is plug-in formulas . Geometry on OA papers is usually right triangles + circles . Memorise the sheet; don't derive under the clock. You're graded on speed: recognise the shape, write the formula, cancel units, pick the option. Diagrams are often drawn to scale — but never tr","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"quant/geometry-and-mensuration.html#formulas","page":"Geometry & mensuration","section":"Formula sheet (learn cold)","tool":"quant","text":"02 Formula sheet (learn cold) Shape Area / volume Also know Rectangle l × b Perimeter 2(l+b) Triangle ½ × base × height Equilateral: (√3/4)a² Circle πr² Circumference 2πr Cuboid Volume l·b·h Surface 2(lb+bh+hl) Cube a³ Surface 6a² Cylinder πr²h Curved surface 2πrh Cone (1/3)πr²h Slant √(r²+h²) Spher","keywords":["formula","sheet","learn","cold"]},{"url":"quant/geometry-and-mensuration.html#traps","page":"Geometry & mensuration","section":"Classic traps","tool":"quant","text":"03 Classic traps Trap Use diameter as radius in πr². Or confuse perimeter with area. Or forget ½ on triangle area. Fix Label r vs d on the diagram. Ask: \"am I measuring edge length or enclosed space?\" For triangles, say \"half base height\" out loud. Common trap gotcha A wire of length L bent into a c","keywords":["classic","traps"]},{"url":"quant/geometry-and-mensuration.html#solve","page":"Geometry & mensuration","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"quant","text":"04 Solve it fast Area of a circle with diameter 14 (use π = 22/7)? warm-up Solve it r = 7. Area = πr² = (22/7)·49 = 22·7 = 154 . The fast way shortcut Halve the diameter first. With π = 22/7 and r = 7, the 7s cancel cleanly. A rectangular field is 40 m by 30 m. Cost of fencing at ₹20/m? core Solve i","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/series-and-analogies.html","page":"Series, analogies & classification","section":"Series, analogies & classification.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Series, analogies & classification. A row of numbers or letters hides one rule — find it and the next term falls out. The whole skill is testing a few suspects fast, not staring and hoping.","keywords":["series","analogies","classification"]},{"url":"reasoning/series-and-analogies.html#idea","page":"Series, analogies & classification","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Every series obeys one hidden rule . Don't guess — write the gaps between terms and read the pattern off: constant difference, a ratio, squares/cubes, or two patterns interleaved. Analogies and odd-one-out are the same move in disguise: name the relationship (or shared property), then ap","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/series-and-analogies.html#number-series","page":"Series, analogies & classification","section":"Number series: write the gaps","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Number series: write the gaps First thing, always: subtract neighbours and write the difference row underneath. If the gaps are equal it's arithmetic ; if each term is a fixed multiple it's geometric (×2, ×3); if the gaps themselves form a pattern (3, 5, 7…), the rule lives one level down. 3 · 6 ","keywords":["number","series","write","the","gaps"]},{"url":"reasoning/series-and-analogies.html#letters-analogies","page":"Series, analogies & classification","section":"Letters, analogies & odd-one-out","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Letters, analogies & odd-one-out Letter series are number series in disguise: convert to positions ( A=1 … Z=26 ) and the shift becomes obvious. For analogies, state the rule in words first; for odd-one-out, find the property four share and one breaks. Type The move Example Letter series Number t","keywords":["letters","analogies","odd","one","out"]},{"url":"reasoning/series-and-analogies.html#solve","page":"Series, analogies & classification","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Solve it fast Next term: 3, 6, 11, 18, ? core Solve it Gaps: 6−3=3, 11−6=5, 18−11=7. The gaps go 3, 5, 7, so the next gap is 9 → 18 + 9 = 27 . The fast way shortcut Don't hunt for a formula — just write the gap row. Consecutive odd numbers (3,5,7,9) is the most common \"growing gap\" pattern there ","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/coding-decoding.html","page":"Coding–decoding","section":"Coding–decoding.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Coding–decoding. A word is scrambled by a secret rule — your job is to crack the rule from one example, then apply it. Once you see the shift, the rest is bookkeeping.","keywords":["coding","decoding"]},{"url":"reasoning/coding-decoding.html#idea","page":"Coding–decoding","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Coding hides a letter ↔ letter rule . Compare each letter of the code with its original, measure the shift , and reuse it. Keep the alphabet positions handy: A=1 … Z=26 . Almost every variant is one of five: shift by n (Caesar), positional value, letter substitution, symbol coding, or nu","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/coding-decoding.html#caesar","page":"Coding–decoding","section":"Letter shift (Caesar): +n with wraparound","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Letter shift (Caesar): +n with wraparound Pick one letter and its code, count the gap — that's n. Apply the same +n to every letter, and when you run off the end, wrap Z → A (Y+3 = B, not \"}\"). Treat the alphabet as a loop, not a line. Shift +3 — every letter slides three steps right A→D, B→E, C→","keywords":["letter","shift","caesar","with","wraparound"]},{"url":"reasoning/coding-decoding.html#positional","page":"Coding–decoding","section":"Positional value & substitution","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Positional value & substitution Many codes just swap each letter for its position number (A=1, B=2, … Z=26) or for another fixed letter/symbol. Spot whether the code is letters, numbers, or symbols, and decode one column at a time. A=1 · B=2 · C=3 · … · M=13 · N=14 · … · Z=26 # memorise the ancho","keywords":["positional","value","substitution"]},{"url":"reasoning/coding-decoding.html#solve","page":"Coding–decoding","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Solve it fast Shift +2: the letter M becomes? warm-up Solve it M is the 13th letter; +2 lands on the 15th = O . (M → N → O, two steps.) The fast way shortcut Count jumps on your fingers — M, then two hops: N, O. For small shifts you never need positions at all. With A=1 … Z=26, what is the code f","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/blood-relations.html","page":"Blood relations","section":"Blood relations.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Blood relations. \"My mother's brother's only daughter\" sounds like a riddle until you draw it. A two-minute family tree turns these into free marks every time.","keywords":["blood","relations"]},{"url":"reasoning/blood-relations.html#idea","page":"Blood relations","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Never track relations in words — draw a family tree . Use □ for male, ○ for female , stack generations as rows, and join them with lines: a vertical line for parent–child, a horizontal one for marriage. Then it's just reading off the picture. Coded versions (\"A + B means A is the father ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/blood-relations.html#tree-method","page":"Blood relations","section":"The family-tree method","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 The family-tree method Put the oldest generation on top , children below, on the same row as their siblings. Marriages sit side-by-side (──), and a line drops to each child. Mark gender with the shape; leave it unmarked if the puzzle never tells you. Three generations — grandparents, parents, chi","keywords":["the","family","tree","method"]},{"url":"reasoning/blood-relations.html#coded-and-photo","page":"Blood relations","section":"Coded relations & the \"photo\" lines","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Coded relations & the \"photo\" lines \"Pointing to a photo\" questions hinge on whose relative is described — anchor the sentence on the speaker , then walk outward. \"His father is my father's son\" with no siblings means the speaker is the father → the boy is his son. Coded clue Means On the tree A ","keywords":["coded","relations","the","photo","lines"]},{"url":"reasoning/blood-relations.html#solve","page":"Blood relations","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Solve it fast A is B's father. B is C's sister. How is A related to C? warm-up Solve it B and C are sisters (same row, shared parents), and A is B's father — so A is also C's father. A is C's father . The fast way shortcut Siblings share both parents. The moment \"B is C's sister\" lands, B and C s","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/direction-sense.html","page":"Direction sense","section":"Direction sense.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Direction sense. Someone walks, turns, and walks again — where do they end up, and how far from the start? Draw it, and these become free marks.","keywords":["direction","sense"]},{"url":"reasoning/direction-sense.html#idea","page":"Direction sense","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Two rules solve almost everything: turning rotates your facing (right = clockwise, left = anti-clockwise), and final distance from start is the straight line — often a 3-4-5 triangle. Never track directions in your head — draw the path . A quick sketch on the rough sheet turns a confusin","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/direction-sense.html#turns","page":"Direction sense","section":"Turning: right is clockwise","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Turning: right is clockwise Face a direction, then turn. Right moves you clockwise (N→E→S→W); left moves anti-clockwise (N→W→S→E). Two turns the same way = a U-turn (180°). The compass — right = clockwise Facing North (accent arrow), a right turn swings you clockwise toward East. Common trap gotc","keywords":["turning","right","is","clockwise"]},{"url":"reasoning/direction-sense.html#displacement","page":"Direction sense","section":"Distance from start = the straight line","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Distance from start = the straight line When they ask \"how far from the starting point\", you want the shortest (straight-line) distance , not the total walked. Add up net North–South and net East–West, then use Pythagoras. Watch for the friendly 3-4-5 and 5-12-13 triples. distance = ( (net N–S) 2","keywords":["distance","from","start","the","straight","line"]},{"url":"reasoning/direction-sense.html#solve","page":"Direction sense","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Solve it fast Facing East, you turn right, then right again. Which way are you facing? warm-up Solve it Right from East → South; right again → West. Two same-direction turns = 180°, so East → West . The fast way shortcut Two turns the same way = a U-turn. Just flip to the opposite direction; skip","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"Syllogisms & statements.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Syllogisms & statements. You're handed statements and a list of conclusions — tick only what must follow. Draw the circles, ignore the real world, and these become reliable marks.","keywords":["syllogisms","statements"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html#idea","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea A syllogism gives you statements (treated as true ) and asks which conclusions are forced . Draw each as overlapping circles (a Venn diagram ) and read off only what every valid picture shares. The skill isn't logic theory — it's translating \"All / Some / No\" into circles, then refusing ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html#venn","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"Turn each statement into circles","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Turn each statement into circles Four statement types, four pictures. All A are B → A sits inside B. No A are B → two separate circles. Some A are B → circles overlap. Some A are not B → part of A sticks out of B. All A are B (nested) vs Some A are B (overlap) \"All A are B\" buries A inside B; \"So","keywords":["turn","each","statement","into","circles"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html#must-vs-maybe","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"\"Definitely true\" vs \"possibly true\"","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 \"Definitely true\" vs \"possibly true\" A conclusion is valid only if it holds in every diagram you can legally draw. If you can sketch even one valid arrangement where it fails, it is not a must — reject it, however likely it feels. Definitely true Holds in all legal pictures. Example: All A are B,","keywords":["definitely","true","vs","possibly"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html#assumptions","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"Statement–conclusion & statement–assumption","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Statement–conclusion & statement–assumption Same rule, two close cousins. Conclusion : does it necessarily follow from the statement? Assumption : is it something the speaker must already be taking for granted for the statement to make sense? Conclusion Accept only if it is forced by the statemen","keywords":["statement","conclusion","assumption"]},{"url":"reasoning/syllogisms-and-statements.html#solve","page":"Syllogisms & statements","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"05 Solve it fast All cats are animals. All animals are mortal. Therefore? warm-up Solve it Nest the circles: cats animals mortal. Anything in the innermost circle is in the outermost, so All cats are mortal . The fast way shortcut Two \"All\" statements chain transitively: All X are Y, all Y are Z → A","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/coded-inequalities.html","page":"Coded inequalities","section":"Coded inequalities.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Coded inequalities. Symbols stand for >, <, ≥, ≤, =. Decode the chain once, then ask: does the conclusion must follow, or only sometimes? Pure pattern speed — high OA yield.","keywords":["coded","inequalities"]},{"url":"reasoning/coded-inequalities.html#idea","page":"Coded inequalities","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea You're given a coding key (e.g. ★ means ≥) and a statement like P ★ Q $ R. Translate to ordinary inequalities, then test conclusions the same way as syllogisms: only tick what is forced . Almost every bank question is: (1) decode, (2) write one combined chain if possible, (3) check each ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/coded-inequalities.html#decode","page":"Coded inequalities","section":"Decode → chain → conclude","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Decode → chain → conclude 1 Write the key Copy ★ = ≥, $ = <, etc. on scratch paper. Don't keep it in your head. 2 Rewrite the statement Replace every symbol. Prefer a single left-to-right chain: P ≥ Q > R = S. 3 Test conclusions For each claim, ask: can I pick numbers that obey the statement but ","keywords":["decode","chain","conclude"]},{"url":"reasoning/coded-inequalities.html#either","page":"Coded inequalities","section":"Either–or and \"not definite\"","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Either–or and \"not definite\" Some option sets include either I or II : exactly one of two conclusions must hold, but you don't know which (classic when A ≥ B and conclusions are A > B vs A = B). Either–or pattern Statement: A ≥ B. Conclusions: (I) A > B (II) A = B. Neither alone is definite, but ","keywords":["either","or","and","not","definite"]},{"url":"reasoning/coded-inequalities.html#solve","page":"Coded inequalities","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Solve it fast Key: ★ means ≥, # means =. Statement: A ★ B # C. Does A ≥ C follow? warm-up Solve it Decode: A ≥ B = C ⇒ A ≥ C. Yes — definite . The fast way shortcut Equality is transparent — swap B with C mentally and keep the ≥. A ≥ B ≥ C. Conclusion: A > C. Follows? core Solve it Counterexample","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"Seating & puzzles.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Seating & puzzles. Five clues, one arrangement — who sits where, or who lives on which floor? Lock the firmest clue first, sketch the rest, and the picture finishes itself.","keywords":["seating","puzzles"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html#idea","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Every arrangement puzzle is a grid you fill from the most certain clue outward . Draw the seats (a row or a circle), place the fixed person, then branch the maybes and eliminate. Don't juggle clues in your head — commit to paper. The order you process clues in matters: definite positions","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html#linear-circular","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"Linear vs circular: the facing flip","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Linear vs circular: the facing flip In a row , everyone faces you, so left/right is fixed. In a circle facing the centre , each person's left is your right — directions are mirrored . Facing outward flips it back. A 5-seat row and a 6-seat circle Row of 5: the middle is seat 3. Circle of 6: the s","keywords":["linear","vs","circular","the","facing","flip"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html#method","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"The method: lock, branch, eliminate","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 The method: lock, branch, eliminate A reliable three-move recipe turns any arrangement into a fill-in-the-blanks exercise. 1 Lock the most-certain clue Find the clue that pins an exact seat (\"A is at the head\", \"C is 3rd\") and write it in. Fixed positions beat relative ones. 2 Branch the possibil","keywords":["the","method","lock","branch","eliminate"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html#floor-order","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"Floor & order puzzles","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Floor & order puzzles Floor puzzles are a vertical row — number them bottom (1) to top (n) and treat \"above / below\" exactly like \"right / left\". Order puzzles (rank, age, height) are the same single line. Anchor Place absolutes first: \"X is on the top floor\", \"the tallest is …\". These are your l","keywords":["floor","order","puzzles"]},{"url":"reasoning/seating-and-puzzles.html#solve","page":"Seating & puzzles","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"05 Solve it fast Five people sit in a row. A is exactly in the middle. A's seat number? warm-up Solve it The middle of an odd row is (n+1)/2. For 5 seats that's seat 3 — two seats on each side. The fast way shortcut Odd row → unique middle at (n+1)/2. Even rows have no single middle, so a \"middle\" c","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice.","tool":"reasoning","text":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice. Four tiny topics, four tiny formulas. Memorise the degree-per-unit and the counting rules, and these turn into instant marks instead of fiddly arithmetic.","keywords":["clocks","calendars","cubes","dice"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#idea","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"The idea","tool":"reasoning","text":"01 The idea Each of these is just steady motion or fixed counting : hands sweep at a constant rate, days roll forward in 7s, and a painted cube's small cubes are sorted by how many faces show. No insight required — pick the right rule, plug numbers, done. The win is recalling the four formulas under","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#clocks","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Clocks: angle = |30H − 5.5M|","tool":"reasoning","text":"02 Clocks: angle = |30H − 5.5M| The minute hand moves 6°/min (360° in 60). The hour hand moves 30°/hr plus 0.5°/min (it creeps as minutes pass). The angle between them collapses to one formula. angle = | 30 H 5.5 M | # 30H from the hour, 5.5M nets both creeps 3:00 — hands 90° apart At 3:00: H = 3, M","keywords":["clocks","angle"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#calendars","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Calendars: odd days mod 7","tool":"reasoning","text":"03 Calendars: odd days mod 7 Days of the week cycle every 7, so only the remainder mod 7 — the \" odd days \" — shifts the weekday. Add the odd days to today's weekday and you've jumped any gap. future day = today + (gap mod 7 ) # 15 days → 15 mod 7 = 1 odd day Odd days The leftover after removing who","keywords":["calendars","odd","days","mod"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#cubes","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Painted cubes: count by faces shown","tool":"reasoning","text":"04 Painted cubes: count by faces shown Paint a big cube, cut it into n×n×n small cubes — then sort the little cubes by how many painted faces they have. The positions never change, so memorise the four counts. Painted faces Where Count 3 faces corners 8 (always) 2 faces edges 12(n−2) 1 face face cen","keywords":["painted","cubes","count","by","faces","shown"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#dice","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Dice: opposite faces","tool":"reasoning","text":"05 Dice: opposite faces On a standard die, opposite faces sum to 7 (1-6, 2-5, 3-4). For \"dice problems\" with two snapshots, the face common to both views can't move, so the two side faces from one view are opposite each other. opposite of x = 7 x # standard die: 1↔6, 2↔5, 3↔4 Common trap gotcha The ","keywords":["dice","opposite","faces"]},{"url":"reasoning/clocks-calendars-cubes-dice.html#solve","page":"Clocks, calendars, cubes & dice","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"reasoning","text":"06 Solve it fast How far does the minute hand move in 20 minutes? warm-up Solve it The minute hand sweeps 6° per minute. 6 × 20 = 120° . The fast way shortcut Minute hand = 6°/min, so 20 min is a third of the dial = 120°. Each 5-minute mark is 30°. A cube painted on all faces is cut into 27 equal cu","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"Reading comprehension.","tool":"verbal","text":"Reading comprehension. Long passage, a clock ticking, four near-identical options. The trick isn't reading faster — it's answering from the text and binning the options that overreach.","keywords":["reading","comprehension"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html#idea","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"The idea","tool":"verbal","text":"01 The idea RC is an evidence hunt , not a memory test. Skim for structure → read the question → locate the line that proves the answer. If you can't point at the sentence, it isn't the answer. You're not graded on understanding every word — you're graded on matching each option to the passage. Map ","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html#attack","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"The attack plan","tool":"verbal","text":"02 The attack plan Do it in this order. Reading the question before hunting saves you from re-reading the passage five times. 1 Skim for structure One pass for the skeleton: what's the topic, where does each paragraph turn, what's the author's stance? Don't memorise details — just build a map. 2 Rea","keywords":["the","attack","plan"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html#eliminate","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"Eliminate the tempting wrong option","tool":"verbal","text":"03 Eliminate the tempting wrong option Most wrong answers fail in one of three ways: extreme (\"always/never/only\"), out of scope (true elsewhere, not in this passage), or distorted (right words, wrong claim). The correct option is usually the cautious, hedged one. Evidence-based (right) \"The author ","keywords":["eliminate","the","tempting","wrong","option"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html#hard","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"Hard stems (OA stretch)","tool":"verbal","text":"04 Hard stems (OA stretch) Harder RC isn't longer vocabulary — it's sneaky stems . Train these four so you don't waste time decoding the question itself. Stem What it really wants Trap \"According to the passage…\" A detail stated in the text An inference that sounds smart but isn't written \"It can be","keywords":["hard","stems","oa","stretch"]},{"url":"verbal/reading-comprehension.html#solve","page":"Reading comprehension","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"verbal","text":"05 Solve it fast What's the best strategy for a timed RC passage? warm-up Solve it Skim for the structure, read the question, then go back and answer from the text — not from memory or outside knowledge. Skim for structure, then answer from the text . The fast way shortcut Map first, hunt second. Th","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"verbal/grammar-and-error-spotting.html","page":"Grammar & error spotting","section":"Grammar & error spotting.","tool":"verbal","text":"Grammar & error spotting. \"Spot the error\" lives off a handful of rules tested again and again. Learn the seven high-frequency traps and you'll catch most of them on sight.","keywords":["grammar","error","spotting"]},{"url":"verbal/grammar-and-error-spotting.html#idea","page":"Grammar & error spotting","section":"The idea","tool":"verbal","text":"01 The idea Error-spotting is pattern recognition , not deep grammar. The same few rules — subject–verb agreement, tense, pronouns, prepositions, comparatives — supply nearly every question. Check the verb against its real subject first. Don't read for \"what sounds off\". Run a fixed checklist over e","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"verbal/grammar-and-error-spotting.html#errors","page":"Grammar & error spotting","section":"The seven high-frequency errors","tool":"verbal","text":"02 The seven high-frequency errors This table is the syllabus. Scan each underlined chunk for these, in roughly this order. Error type Wrong Right Subject–verb agreement The list of items are long. The list of items is long. (subject = list) Tense consistency He came in and sits down. He came in and","keywords":["the","seven","high","frequency","errors"]},{"url":"verbal/grammar-and-error-spotting.html#agreement","page":"Grammar & error spotting","section":"The agreement traps that catch everyone","tool":"verbal","text":"03 The agreement traps that catch everyone Two patterns supply a huge share of marks: a collective noun acting as one unit takes a singular verb, and \"one of the X who/that…\" uses a plural verb (it refers to the X's, not \"one\"). Reads fine, but wrong \"The committee are deciding.\" · \"He is one of tho","keywords":["the","agreement","traps","that","catch","everyone"]},{"url":"verbal/grammar-and-error-spotting.html#solve","page":"Grammar & error spotting","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"verbal","text":"04 Solve it fast \"He is taller ___ his brother.\" Fill the blank. warm-up Solve it Comparisons take than (not \"then\", which is about time). than . The fast way shortcut See \"-er\" or \"more\" → expect than . \"Then\" = next-in-time; never use it after a comparative. Spot the error: \"Each of the boys have ","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"verbal/sentence-completion-and-para-jumbles.html","page":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles","section":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles.","tool":"verbal","text":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles. Both reward one habit: read the joining words. A connector like \"but\" or \"because\" tells you the answer before you've weighed the options — and the same logic links scrambled sentences into order.","keywords":["sentence","completion","para","jumbles"]},{"url":"verbal/sentence-completion-and-para-jumbles.html#idea","page":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles","section":"The idea","tool":"verbal","text":"01 The idea The connector decides the direction . Contrast (\"but / despite / although\") needs an opposite ; cause (\"because / so\") needs a consequence ; addition (\"and / moreover\") needs more of the same . Match the word to the logic, not the vibe. For jumbles, the same links order the sentences: fi","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"verbal/sentence-completion-and-para-jumbles.html#connectors","page":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles","section":"Fill-in-the-blank: read the connector","tool":"verbal","text":"02 Fill-in-the-blank: read the connector Spot the signpost first, then pick the word whose direction matches. Collocations (fixed word pairings) settle most ties. Connector Signals Blank should be… but, yet, however, despite, although Contrast The opposite of the other clause because, since, as, so,","keywords":["fill","in","the","blank","read","connector"]},{"url":"verbal/sentence-completion-and-para-jumbles.html#jumbles","page":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles","section":"Para-jumbles: find the opener, then chain","tool":"verbal","text":"03 Para-jumbles: find the opener, then chain Don't try all orderings. Find the sentence that stands alone (no \"this / it / however\" pointing backward), then thread the rest by their links. 1 Find the opener It introduces the topic with a full noun and no backward reference. A sentence starting \"This","keywords":["para","jumbles","find","the","opener","then","chain"]},{"url":"verbal/sentence-completion-and-para-jumbles.html#solve","page":"Sentence completion & para-jumbles","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"verbal","text":"04 Solve it fast \"Despite the rain, they ___ the match.\" Fill the blank. warm-up Solve it \"Despite\" signals contrast — rain would normally stop play, so they did the opposite and kept going. continued . The fast way shortcut \"Despite / in spite of\" = expect the surprising, against-the-grain choice. ","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"verbal/vocabulary.html","page":"Vocabulary","section":"Vocabulary.","tool":"verbal","text":"Vocabulary. You'll always meet a word you haven't memorised. The win is decoding it on the spot — from its root, its affixes, and the sentence around it — instead of betting on a hunch.","keywords":["vocabulary"]},{"url":"verbal/vocabulary.html#idea","page":"Vocabulary","section":"The idea","tool":"verbal","text":"01 The idea Don't memorise a dictionary — decode . Most hard words are root + affix : \"bene-\" = good, \"-vore\" = eater, \"-phile\" = lover. Crack the parts and the meaning falls out, even for a brand-new word. When the root doesn't help, lean on context (the sentence's drift) and connotation (does it f","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"verbal/vocabulary.html#roots","page":"Vocabulary","section":"The roots & affixes that pay off","tool":"verbal","text":"02 The roots & affixes that pay off A small set unlocks a big slice of the test. Learn these, then build outward. Root / affix Meaning Example un- / in- / a- not, without un known, in visible, a typical bene- / mal- good / bad bene fit, mal function -vore eater of omni vore (eats all), carni vore -p","keywords":["the","roots","affixes","that","pay","off"]},{"url":"verbal/vocabulary.html#connotation","page":"Vocabulary","section":"Context & connotation: the tie-breaker","tool":"verbal","text":"03 Context & connotation: the tie-breaker When two options look close, ask: does the sentence want a positive or negative word? Connotation alone kills the wrong-shade option. Beware false friends — look-alikes that mean different things. Right shade \"The frugal shopper saved money.\" Frugal = thrift","keywords":["context","connotation","the","tie","breaker"]},{"url":"verbal/vocabulary.html#solve","page":"Vocabulary","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"verbal","text":"04 Solve it fast Synonym of \"abundant\"? warm-up Solve it Abundant means present in large quantity — i.e. plentiful. plentiful . The fast way shortcut Sort by connotation: \"abundant\" is a \"lots of it\" word, so drop the \"little\" options (scarce, tiny, rare) in one sweep. Plentiful is the only \"plenty\"","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"di/charts-and-tables.html","page":"Charts & tables","section":"Charts & tables.","tool":"di","text":"Charts & tables. A wall of numbers with five quick questions hanging off it. The marks aren't in the maths — they're in reading the right cell and approximating before you compute.","keywords":["charts","tables"]},{"url":"di/charts-and-tables.html#idea","page":"Charts & tables","section":"The idea","tool":"di","text":"01 The idea DI is reading + one quick calc , not heavy arithmetic. Lock onto the labels, units and scale first; then most answers are a single percentage, ratio or difference. Tables give exact values; bars compare sizes; lines show a trend over time; pies split one whole into shares. Same skill eve","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"di/charts-and-tables.html#charts","page":"Charts & tables","section":"Bars, lines & the pie formula","tool":"di","text":"02 Bars, lines & the pie formula A bar chart compares quantities side by side — read each bar off the y-axis. A line chart is the same data joined up to show rise/fall over time. A pie splits 100% (= 360°) into slices. Units sold (₹000s) — read the y-axis, don't eyeball Four quarters: 40, 60, 80, 20","keywords":["bars","lines","the","pie","formula"]},{"url":"di/charts-and-tables.html#fast-reads","page":"Charts & tables","section":"Fast reads: percentage, ratio & approximation","tool":"di","text":"03 Fast reads: percentage, ratio & approximation Speed comes from rough-first arithmetic and clean fractions. Round to friendly numbers, get the ballpark, then refine only if two options are close. Slow way \"What % is 396 of 1980?\" → long-divide 396 ÷ 1980 to four decimals, panic at the clock. Fast ","keywords":["fast","reads","percentage","ratio","approximation"]},{"url":"di/charts-and-tables.html#solve","page":"Charts & tables","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"di","text":"04 Solve it fast On a pie chart, a category is 25% of spending. How many degrees is its slice? warm-up Solve it Degrees = share% × 3.6 = 25 × 3.6 = 90° . The fast way shortcut Anchor a few: 25% = 90°, 50% = 180°, 10% = 36°. Most slices are just sums of these. Four bars read 40, 60, 80 and 20 (in tho","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"di/caselets-and-mixed-sets.html","page":"Caselets & mixed sets","section":"Caselets & mixed sets.","tool":"di","text":"Caselets & mixed sets. The same DI maths, but the data is buried in a paragraph instead of a tidy chart. Win these by doing one thing first: turn the prose into a small table.","keywords":["caselets","mixed","sets"]},{"url":"di/caselets-and-mixed-sets.html#idea","page":"Caselets & mixed sets","section":"The idea","tool":"di","text":"01 The idea A caselet is data hidden in sentences . Don't answer from the prose — extract every number into a tiny table , then read it like any other DI set. Mixed sets link several charts/tables about one scenario, so a value from chart A feeds a question on chart B. Build the table once and every","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"di/caselets-and-mixed-sets.html#prose-to-table","page":"Caselets & mixed sets","section":"Prose → table, every time","tool":"di","text":"02 Prose → table, every time Read once with a pen. Each sentence drops one number into a row/column. The moment the table is full, the questions answer themselves. 1 Name the rows & columns Spot the two dimensions — usually a category (cities, years, products) and a metric (count, revenue, %). Sketc","keywords":["prose","table","every","time"]},{"url":"di/caselets-and-mixed-sets.html#mixed","page":"Caselets & mixed sets","section":"Multi-set DI (two charts, one story)","tool":"di","text":"03 Multi-set DI (two charts, one story) Hard OA DI rarely is one lonely bar chart. You get a table + pie , or two years of bars , and Q3 needs a number from chart A times a % from chart B. Treat it as one shared workbook. 1 Find the bridge key What links the charts? Usually a year, a product, or a r","keywords":["multi","set","di","two","charts","one","story"]},{"url":"di/caselets-and-mixed-sets.html#solve","page":"Caselets & mixed sets","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"di","text":"04 Solve it fast A group of 200 is 60% male. How many females? warm-up Solve it Female share = 100% − 60% = 40%; 40% of 200 = 80 . The fast way shortcut Go straight to the complement: 40% = 2/5, and 2/5 of 200 = 80. Don't find the 120 males first. A firm reports revenue 500 and cost 350. What is the","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"di/data-sufficiency.html","page":"Data sufficiency","section":"Data sufficiency.","tool":"di","text":"Data sufficiency. A weird-looking format that's actually a gift: you don't have to find the answer, only decide whether the clues are enough to find it. Judge, don't solve.","keywords":["data","sufficiency"]},{"url":"di/data-sufficiency.html#idea","page":"Data sufficiency","section":"The idea","tool":"di","text":"01 The idea You get a question plus two statements, (1) and (2) . Your job is to decide if they're SUFFICIENT to pin down a unique answer — not to compute the value. The five answer choices are always the same: (1) alone, (2) alone, both together, either alone, or neither. Test each statement on its","keywords":["the","idea"]},{"url":"di/data-sufficiency.html#five-way","page":"Data sufficiency","section":"The five-way logic & decision path","tool":"di","text":"02 The five-way logic & decision path Always check (1) alone, then (2) alone, then (combine only if both failed). The branch you land on names the answer. Decision tree — test alone, then together Five outcomes: (1) only · (2) only · either alone · both needed · neither. Stop at the first branch tha","keywords":["the","five","way","logic","decision","path"]},{"url":"di/data-sufficiency.html#approach","page":"Data sufficiency","section":"A clean approach","tool":"di","text":"03 A clean approach Hide statement (2), judge (1); hide (1), judge (2). Combine only if both alone failed. \"Could the answer be two different values?\" → insufficient. Slow / wrong Grind both statements into the full numeric answer, then second-guess which choice to pick — burning a minute you don't ","keywords":["clean","approach"]},{"url":"di/data-sufficiency.html#solve","page":"Data sufficiency","section":"Solve it fast","tool":"di","text":"04 Solve it fast In data sufficiency, what are you actually deciding? warm-up Solve it Whether the statements are sufficient to answer — not the final numeric value itself. The fast way shortcut Reframe every DS question as \" can I answer? \", never \" what is the answer? \". That single switch saves t","keywords":["solve","it","fast"]},{"url":"practice/method.html","page":"How to crack the test","section":"How to crack the test.","tool":"practice","text":"How to crack the test. Aptitude rounds reward a plan , not heroics. Your score is attempts × accuracy under a hard clock — so the game is banking easy marks fast and never letting one stubborn question sink a whole section.","keywords":["how","to","crack","the","test"]},{"url":"practice/method.html#idea","page":"How to crack the test","section":"Score = attempts × accuracy","tool":"practice","text":"01 Score = attempts × accuracy You don't need every question — you need the most marks per minute . Attempt the easy ones quickly and accurately; leave the time-sinks for a second pass. In plain English analogy It's a supermarket dash with a timer. You don't walk every aisle — you grab the cheap, ea","keywords":["score","attempts","accuracy"]},{"url":"practice/method.html#budget","page":"How to crack the test","section":"Spend time like a budget","tool":"practice","text":"02 Spend time like a budget Tests vary, but the shape is similar: roughly 40–60 seconds per question . Give each section a fixed slice and move on when it's spent — an unanswered easy question in the next section is worth more than a hard one you're still fighting. Section Typical Q Typical time ≈ p","keywords":["spend","time","like","budget"]},{"url":"practice/method.html#accuracy","page":"How to crack the test","section":"Protect your accuracy","tool":"practice","text":"03 Protect your accuracy Speed without accuracy just collects penalties. The fastest way to be accurate is to recognise the type and apply the shortcut — which is exactly what the topic pages drill into you. Common trap gotcha The sunk-cost trap: you've spent two minutes, so you spend two more \"to n","keywords":["protect","your","accuracy"]},{"url":"practice/method.html#solve","page":"How to crack the test","section":"Your prep playbook","tool":"practice","text":"04 Your prep playbook How should I actually use this guide? warm-up The plan Read a topic page for the shortcut, do its 5-question mini-drill until you're quick, then come to the Timed drill and mix sections. Use the formula sheet and flashcards for daily recall. The fast way shortcut Learn → drill ","keywords":["your","prep","playbook"]},{"url":"practice/drill.html","page":"Timed practice drill","section":"Timed practice drill.","tool":"practice","text":"Timed practice drill. The whole point of aptitude prep: answer under the clock . Pick a section (or mix everything), choose how many questions, and race — you get instant scoring, a per-question review with worked explanations, and your best score saved.","keywords":["timed","practice","drill"]},{"url":"practice/drill.html#how","page":"Timed practice drill","section":"How it works","tool":"practice","text":"01 How it works 1 Pick a section & count All sections mixed, Quant / Reasoning / Verbal / DI, or Stretch only for the harder OA pool. Choose 10, 20, or 30 questions. 2 Race the clock About 45 seconds per question. The timer turns amber, then red — practising under pressure is the point. 3 Answer & l","keywords":["how","it","works"]},{"url":"practice/drill.html#arena","page":"Timed practice drill","section":"The arena","tool":"practice","text":"02 The arena Open this page in a browser to run the timed drill — choose a section and count, answer against the clock, and get an instant score with explanations. (JavaScript required for the live timer.)","keywords":["the","arena"]},{"url":"practice/drill.html#solve","page":"Timed practice drill","section":"Practise like it counts","tool":"practice","text":"03 Practise like it counts Should I review the misses or just keep drilling? core Do this Always open the review and read the explanation for every wrong answer — the gain is in understanding the trap, not in raw reps. Then re-drill the same topic page until those misses turn green. The fast way sho","keywords":["practise","like","it","counts"]},{"url":"reference/formula-sheet.html","page":"Formula & shortcut sheet","section":"Formula & shortcut sheet.","tool":"reference","text":"Formula & shortcut sheet. Every formula and trick in the guide on one page — the night-before refresher. Skim it until each line triggers the method on sight.","keywords":["formula","shortcut","sheet"]},{"url":"reference/formula-sheet.html#quant","page":"Formula & shortcut sheet","section":"Quantitative","tool":"reference","text":"01 Quantitative Percentages x% of y (x/100)·y = y% of x Change (new − old)/old × 100 Successive multiply factors: ×(1±a/100) Up a% then down a% net −(a²/100)% ½=50 · ¼=25 · ⅕=20 · ⅛=12.5 · ⅓=33⅓ · ⅙=16⅔ (%) Profit & interest Profit% (SP − CP)/CP × 100 Discount on Marked Price, not CP SI P·R·T / 100 ","keywords":["quantitative"]},{"url":"reference/formula-sheet.html#reasoning","page":"Formula & shortcut sheet","section":"Reasoning & data interpretation","tool":"reference","text":"02 Reasoning & data interpretation Clocks & calendars Minute hand 6° per minute Hour hand 30°/hr + 0.5°/min Angle |30H − 5.5M| Calendar odd days = days mod 7 Cubes & dice Painted cube (n³) 3 faces: 8 corners 2 faces: 12(n−2) edges 1 face: 6(n−2)² Dice opposite faces sum 7 Syllogism & directions Meth","keywords":["reasoning","data","interpretation"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html","page":"Flashcards","section":"Flashcards.","tool":"reference","text":"Flashcards. Active recall beats re-reading. Flip a card, say the answer before you check, and mark it Known or Review — your progress saves in this browser. Shuffle puts the ones you still miss first.","keywords":["flashcards"]},{"url":"reference/flashcards.html#cards","page":"Flashcards","section":"The deck","tool":"reference","text":"01 The deck Twenty high-yield conversions, formulas, and method cues. Drill them daily until each front instantly fires the back. 0 / 0 known Shuffle Reset 12.5% as a fraction? 1/8 — divide by 8. Known Review 33⅓% as a fraction? 1/3. Known Review Up a%, then down a% — net change? A loss of (a²/100)%","keywords":["the","deck"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html","page":"Glossary","section":"Glossary.","tool":"reference","text":"Glossary. The aptitude & reasoning vocabulary, A–Z — the terms that show up in question stems and instructions.","keywords":["glossary"]},{"url":"reference/glossary.html#az","page":"Glossary","section":"A–Z","tool":"reference","text":"01 A–Z Alligation A shortcut for mixtures and weighted averages: the ratio of two ingredients equals the cross-differences of their values from the mean — cheaper : dearer = (dearer − mean) : (mean − cheaper). Analogy A \"A : B :: C : ?\" question — identify the relationship between the first pair and","keywords":[]}];
